Z||000000||MCFIEROGUY||09-02-2000||11:24 PM||||86 SE V6 Auto <BR>mess of a car (see new to forum topic)<BR>I have it running now by doing major tune up but it runs harshly to say the least: Idle speed is eratic, knocking when above 2000rpm and increasing from that point, oil pressure gauge reacts in sync with the rpms, no smoke <BR>at all from exhaust, engine seems to run very hot but temp gauge does not register at all. <BR>   If any one has any suggestions about where to start to diagnose and solve my problem, I would be greatly appreciative <BR>thanks.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||09-02-2000||11:27 PM||||check and reset your timing. were there previously set codes that your aware of?||||reg||1
Z||000002||MCFIEROGUY||09-02-2000||11:42 PM||||right I have not done the timing on it yet but I am quite the novice, but eager to learn! Do you really think the timing could cause all that trouble?<P>------------------<BR>-Chris||||reg||1
Z||000003||AL||09-03-2000||03:49 AM||||first i would pull the wires of the temp sensor,under the coil,then i would touch the wires (ground them)if the needle pegs or moves when you ground them then change the sensor,next, check or change your coolant if it looks good then i would check the timing,if you haven't done a tune up on the car now is the time before you check timing,after the tune up check fr vacuam leaks in the little hoses under the plenum,if no leaks are found then check the timing and set it.also while your doing all this change the fuel filter also andyour car should work right,good luck.<B>AL</B> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AL_sig.gif"> <P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||09-03-2000||11:15 AM||||What Al just said only works for gauge senders. Gauge sensors don't affect performance. They are not connected to the ECM at all. Don't try this one unless you are sure what wire you are messing with. If you ground the wrong sensor wiring you can fry the ECM. <P>Do check the plug on the ECM coolant sensor. Not sure where it is on V6. The connector has a habbit of getting crud in it. If the connector is bad get a Borg Warner replacement sensor. They come with the new type connector and instructions to use it.<P>You need to check timing. You should also disconnect the Tach Filter just to eliminate the posibility it's part of the problem. (That thing is a real piece of junk. I'm so glad I don't have one.)<P>You need to look for vacuum leaks, especially in the EGR and PCV systems. <P>Also check the line for the MAP sensor. If it's leaking blocked or connectted to the wrong port it will affect timing. The MAP sensor is used to calculate "vacuum advance" for ignition amongst other things.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Luperman||09-03-2000||11:17 PM||||Your ecm coolant sensor on the V-6 is located above the water pump and below the water neck on the side of the engine the sensor is mounted horizontaly in the engine yellow and black wire.<P>------------------<BR>86 fiero 2M6 V-6<BR>84 Grand Wagoneer 360||||reg||1
